  "guidebook": {
    "intro": "You hope never to need these words, but knowing them makes you calmer when something goes wrong.",
    "phrases": [
      { "target": "Kumetokea ajali!", "base": "There has been an accident!" },
      { "target": "Piga simu polisi!", "base": "Call the police!" },
      { "target": "Tunataka gari la wagonjwa!", "base": "We need an ambulance!" },
      { "target": "Usiogope, uko salama.", "base": "Don’t be afraid, you’re safe." }
    ],
    "tips": [
      {
        "id": "ku-there",
        "title": "Something happened: ku-",
        "body": [
          "For events with no clear subject, Swahili uses the place prefix **ku-**: **kumetokea ajali** (there has been an accident), **kuna moto** (there is a fire).",
          "Changes that happen to someone often take **-wa** (the passive) or a stative ending: **ameibiwa** (has been robbed), **ameumia** (is hurt)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "usi-dont",
        "title": "Don’t: usi- … -e",
        "body": [
          "To tell someone not to do something, use **usi-** and change the last **-a** to **-e**: **ogopa** (fear) → **usiogope** (don’t be afraid), **kimbia** (run) → **usikimbie** (don’t run).",
          "You already know **Usijali** (don’t worry). Talking to several people, use **msi-**: **msiogope**."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "culture": [
      {
        "title": "Neighbours first",
        "body": [
          "In an emergency the first help usually comes from the people around you: neighbours, shopkeepers and boda boda riders who carry the injured to hospital. Emergency numbers differ from country to country, so save the local ones when you arrive."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "stories": [
